### Paper 1: A Survey on Evaluation of Large Language Models (2023)

**Authors:** Yupeng Chang et al.

**Description:**

This research paper discusses different methods used to evaluate Large Language Models. It explains how LLMs can be tested based on their accuracy, reasoning abilities, performance and reliability. The study also highlights the importance of evaluating language models across different real-world applications.

**Relevance to Our Project:**

This paper helps us understand the importance of evaluating AI-generated responses. It supports our idea of including response-quality evaluation and performance analysis in the observability dashboard.

### Paper 2: AI Observability for Large Language Model Systems: A Multi-Layer Analysis of Monitoring Approaches from Confidence Calibration to Infrastructure Tracing (2026)

**Author:** Twinkll Sisodia

**Description:**

This paper explores different approaches to monitoring Large Language Model systems. It discusses monitoring techniques at different levels, including model confidence, internal model behaviour and infrastructure-level tracing. It also explains the challenges involved in combining different monitoring methods into a single observability system.

**Relevance to Our Project:**

This research is closely related to our project because it highlights the importance of monitoring LLM applications at different levels. It supports our objective of developing a centralized dashboard to display important monitoring information.

### Paper 3: TRAIL: Trace Reasoning and Agentic Issue Localization (2025)

**Authors:** Darshan Deshpande et al.

**Description:**

This research focuses on analyzing execution traces generated by AI agent workflows. It explains the difficulties involved in identifying errors in complex AI applications and introduces a dataset containing annotated traces for studying these problems. The paper highlights how trace analysis can help developers understand failures in AI workflows.

**Relevance to Our Project:**

This paper supports the tracing and debugging part of our project. By using Langfuse tracing, we aim to record and analyze LLM execution activities, helping developers identify errors and understand application behaviour.
